# tabs.zd — one panel at a time, and an honest account of what that is.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS NOT AN ARIA TABLIST, AND IT DOES NOT CLAIM TO BE.
#
# The ARIA tabs pattern is `role="tablist"`, `role="tab"` with
# `aria-selected` on every one of them, `aria-controls` pointing at a
# `role="tabpanel"` with `aria-labelledby` pointing back, and a roving
# `tabindex` so the strip is one tab stop that the arrow keys move within.
# Of those seven attributes the vocabulary can spell exactly one: `role`.
# `aria-selected` and the rest are hyphenated, and §16.3.6 records why that
# is not an oversight — an argument name is a UAX#31 identifier, which
# admits no hyphen, so `aria-selected` is not merely absent but unspellable.
# `tabindex` is absent from the closed argument set too.
#
# A `role="tab"` with no `aria-selected` is worse than no role at all: the
# reader is told the control is a tab and never told whether it is the
# selected one. So this file writes no ARIA roles. What it emits is a row
# of ordinary `Button`s and a region that swaps — which is fully operable
# from the keyboard, because a `button` is, and is announced accurately,
# because it is announced as what it is.
#
# What is lost against a real tablist: the strip is one tab stop per tab
# rather than one in total, and the arrow keys do nothing. Arrow keys are
# not wired here on purpose. Moving *selection* with an arrow while focus
# stays behind — which is all this language can do, since nothing can move
# focus — leaves the focused button and the selected panel disagreeing, and
# a keyboard user then presses Enter and watches the selection jump back.
# That is not a partial implementation of the pattern, it is a different
# and worse one.

# The strip.
#
# `labels` is a `List of Text` and `selected` is the caller's `Whole`
# signal holding the position of the open panel. The pairing of a label
# with its position is `zip` over `indices`, because `each … in` binds the
# element and not where it came from, and a tab needs both: the label to
# show and the index to write.
#
# Three classes rather than one. `class` dresses the strip; `tabClass` and
# `currentClass` are what let a caller show *which* tab is open, and
# without the second of them the selected state would be visible only in
# the panel below. That is class plumbing and not a look: this file still
# ships no colour.
component Tabs with labels, selected, class, tabClass, currentClass
    Row class is class
        each pair in (zip with left is (indices of (listLength of labels)), right is labels)
            Tab pair.second, index is pair.first, selected is selected, tabClass is tabClass, currentClass is currentClass

# One tab. Two `Button`s under an `if` rather than one with a computed
# class, following `examples/todo.zd`: the signal graph swaps the branches
# when `selected` changes and costs one anchor pair per tab that does not.
component Tab with label, index, selected, tabClass, currentClass
    if index is selected
        Button label, class is currentClass
            on click
                set selected to index
    otherwise
        Button label, class is tabClass
            on click
                set selected to index

# The body of one tab, shown when it is the selected one.
#
# The panel is a sibling of the strip rather than something `Tabs` wraps,
# because a component places its `children` in one slot and a tab set has
# as many bodies as it has tabs. The caller writes one `TabPanel` per tab
# and gives each the index it belongs to, which is also the only way the
# two can be kept in step by the compiler rather than by a convention.
component TabPanel with index, selected, class, children
    if index is selected
        Column class is class
            children
